{:edb/db-before-t 1993 :edb/db-after-t 1994 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535306 50 #inst "2026-10-08T21:44:17.764Z" 13194139535306 true] [17592186124152 1100 "$$\nx(x-2)(x+1)y'' + x(x-2)y' + \\dfrac{\\sin x}{(x-2)^2}\\, y = 0\n$$\n\nGiven the points $x = -1,$ $x = 0,$ $x = 1,$ and $x = 2,$ indicate whether they are ordinary or singular points of the differential equation above. If they are singular points, indicate whether they are regular or irregular." 13194139535306 false] [17592186124152 1100 "$$\nx(x-2)(x+1)y'' + x(x-2)y' + \\dfrac{\\sin x}{(x-2)^2}\\, y = 0\n$$\n\nGiven the points $x = -1,$ $x = 0,$ $x = 1,$ and $x = 2,$ indicate whether they are ordinary or singular points of the differential equation above. If they are singular points, indicate whether they are regular or irregular.\n\nClassify using the normalized coefficients after analytically continuing any removable coefficient singularities." 13194139535306 true] [17592186124152 1145 "Normalize the equation to $y''+P(x)y'+Q(x)y=0$, where\n\n$$P(x)=\\frac1{x+1},\\qquad Q(x)=\\frac{\\sin x/x}{(x+1)(x-2)^3}.$$\n\nContinue removable coefficient singularities analytically before classifying. The function $\\sin x/x$ extends to the entire function $\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}(-1)^n x^{2n}/(2n+1)!$, with value $1$ at zero. Thus $P(0)=1$ and $Q(0)=-1/8$, and both coefficients are analytic near zero. The point $x=0$ is ordinary. Both coefficients are also analytic at $x=1$, so that point is ordinary.\n\nAt $x=-1$, $P$ has a pole, while $(x+1)P=1$ and $(x+1)^2Q=(x+1)\\sin x/[x(x-2)^3]$ are analytic. Therefore $x=-1$ is regular singular.\n\nAt $x=2$, $(x-2)P$ is analytic, but $(x-2)^2Q=\\sin x/[x(x+1)(x-2)]$ has a pole because $\\sin2\\ne0$. Therefore $x=2$ is irregular singular." 13194139535306 true] [17592186124152 1145 "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\ny'' + P(x)y' + Q(x)y = f(x)\n$$\n\nif both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nA singular point $x=a$ is regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^2Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $x(x-2)(x+1){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(x-2)(x+1){y}^{″}+x(x-2){y}^{′}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{(x-2)^{2}}y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{x+1}{y}^{′}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x(x+1)(x-2)^{3}}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\dfrac{1}{x+1}\n\n\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n\nQ(x)=\\dfrac{\\sin x}{x(x+1)(x-2)^3}.\n$$\n\nThe function $P(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where $x+1 = 0,$ which occurs at $x = -1.$\n\nThe function $Q(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where $x(x+1)(x-2)^3 = 0,$ which occurs at $x=-1,$ $x=0,$ and $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, $x=1$ is a *ordinary point* of the differential equation, and the *singular points* are\n\n$$\nx=-1, \\quad x=0, \\quad x=2.\n$$\n\nNow, let's classify each singular point.\n\n- For $x=-1,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x+1)P(x) = (x+1) \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{x+1} = 1,\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=-1,$ and the function\n\n$$\n(x+1)^2 Q(x) = (x+1)^2 \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sin x}{x(x+1)(x-2)^3} = \\dfrac{(x+1)\\sin{x}}{x(x-2)^3},\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=-1.$ Thus, $x=-1$ is a *regular singular point* of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=0,$ the function\n\n$$\nxP(x) = x\\cdot\\dfrac{1}{x+1} = \\dfrac{x}{x+1},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=0,$ and the function\n\n$$\nx^2Q(x) = x^2\\cdot\\dfrac{\\sin x}{x(x+1)(x-2)^3} = \\dfrac{x\\sin x}{(x+1)(x-2)^3},\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=0.$ Thus, $x=0$ is a *regular singular point* of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=2,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x-2)P(x) = (x-2)\\cdot\\dfrac{1}{x+1} = \\dfrac{x-2}{x+1},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=2,$ but the function\n\n$$\n(x-2)^2Q(x) = (x-2)^2\\cdot\\dfrac{\\sin x}{x(x+1)(x-2)^3} = \\dfrac{\\sin x}{x(x+1)(x-2)},\n$$\n\nis *not* analytic about $x=2.$ Therefore, $x=2$ is an *irregular singular point* of the differential equation." 13194139535306 false]] :edb/tempids {} :edb/committed false :edb/speculative true}
